course: dictionary
lesson: 6548
title: News: new information
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: News means new information about something that has just happened. «Wait for trusted news before believing that a famous person has died» shows that news is information you can check, and that a trusted source is the best place to find it.
explain: News comes in many places: on TV, on the radio, on websites and in newspapers. «The six o'clock news» is a programme that reports recent events. The word "the" shows that this means one special programme, not news in general.
explain: Some news is not true. «Headlines are written to grab you, so read the story underneath; a shocking post that is on no trusted news site is probably fake.» Fake news is false information that pretends to be real, so always check before you share.
fact: News means new information about something that has just happened.
fact: «Good news» and «bad news» are both news; the words good or bad tell you how the news makes people feel.
fact: «That's news to me» means I didn't know that before.
fact: «Fake news» is false information, so check a trusted news site before you share a story.
fact: News is uncountable: say «some news», not «a news» or «two news».
exercise: «Animal stories are some of the most shared news in the world.» What is news in this sentence? | *Reports of recent events, such as stories about animals | A kind of tree | A type of animal | A place to buy food
exercise: Tom says, «That's news to me!» What does he mean? | *I didn't know that before | I want to read the newspaper now | The news on TV is very loud | I already knew that well
exercise: Why should you check a shocking post before you share it? | Because shocking posts are always true | *Because it may be fake news, so check it on a trusted site | Because sharing is free | Because posts are always written by famous people
test: What does «break the news» mean? | *Tell someone bad news | Write a newspaper | Make a loud noise | Forget something
test: Why do we check news before believing it? | *Because some news is fake, so trusted sources help us tell what is true | Because news never changes | Because news is only for grown-ups | Because all news is always false
exam: Which sentence uses news correctly? | *«I read some news today.» | «I read two news today.» | «I read many news today.» | «I read a news today.»
